entry uranium-hexafluoride
term: Uranium Hexafluoride (UF₆)
difficulty: advanced
image: ../../assets/images/terms/Uranium Hexafluoride (UF₆).webp
related_terms: enrichment
related_terms: uranium-235
related_terms: yellowcake
related_terms: uranium-238
quick_facts: Sublimes at 56 °C — the working gas of enrichment
quick_facts: Only volatile uranium compound suitable for centrifuges
quick_facts: Reacts violently with water → hydrofluoric acid
quick_facts: Shipped internationally in thick-walled steel cylinders
quick_facts: Fluorine comes back out unchanged — reused endlessly

sources:
    {"organization":"World Nuclear","title":"Conversion and Deconversion","url":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/nuclear-fuel-cycle/enrichment-and-conversion"}

definition:
    One uranium atom bonded to six fluorines — the compound that makes enrichment practical. It sublimes directly from solid to gas at just 56 °C under normal pressure, letting centrifuge cascades work on a gas whose molecules differ only by the whisper of mass between U-235 and U-238. The trade-off: UF₆ is ferociously reactive with moisture, breaking down into corrosive hydrofluoric acid, so it travels in rugged cylinders under strict handling rules.
